 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to increasing the\nbonding authority of the New York city housing development corporation",
    "basePrintNo": "S6545",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6545-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §656, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6545\n\nSPONSOR: JACKSON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the private housing finance law, in\nrelation to increasing the bonding authority of the New York city hous-\ning development corporation\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis proposed legislation would amend section 656 of the Private Housing\nFinance Law to allow the New York City Housing Development Corporation\n(\"HDC\") to increase the maximum aggregate principal amount of its\noutstanding notes and bonds from $13.5 billion to $14.5 billion.\n\n \nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\n\nBy the end of April 2019, HDC had approximately $12.3 billion of bonds\nsold or outstanding. HDC expects to issue approximately $600 million for\nits June issuance. After June, HDC would have less than $600 million of\nunused bonding authority. HDC expects to issue approximately $350\nmillion in both tax-exempt and taxable bonds over the next year to\npreserve and refinance a portion of its Mitchell-Lama portfolios; to\nsupport the Neighborhood Pillars program, a new initiative under the\nHousing New York plan to provide financing for nonprofit organizations\nto acquire and preserve affordable housing in existing buildings; and\nfor other preservation projects in the HDC portfolio. In addition, HDC\nexpects to provide financing for numerous new construction low income\nprojects as well as multi-year financing for several large-scale mixed-\nincome developments using both tax-exempt and taxable bonds totaling\napproximately $700 million. Furthermore, HDC may use taxable or recycled\nbonds totaling approximately $200 million in the next year to replace\nthe financing for FHA Risk-Sharing loans provided by the Federal Financ-\ning Bank (as such financing authority failed to be extended by the\nFederal government). In a little over a decade, since January 1, 2005,\nHDC has issued in excess of $20.8 billion in bonds to finance the\nconstruction or preservation of over 158,000 apartments throughout New\nYork City. These include bonds to finance the preservation of thousands\nof Mitchell-Lama apartments as well as Liberty Bonds to finance the\nconstruction or conversion from non-residential use of thousands of\napartments in lower Manhattan.  This record has made HDC one of the\nlargest issuers of multifamily affordable housing bonds in the nation,\nand frequently the top annual issuer.\n\nUnder the City's \"Housing New York\" plan, as of May 1, 2019, HDC has\nfinanced the construction or preservation of more than 59,150 affordable\napartments in less than five years. HDC financed over 76,000 affordable\napartments under the City's prior housing plan, far surpassing its\noriginal goals. HDC financing relating to the City's housing plan is\nprovided under a variety of new programs: (1) the Extremely Low and\nLow-Income Affordable program (\"ELLA\"), which provides apartments for\nlow-income people earning 60% of the Area Median Income (\"AMI\") or less,\nincluding deeply affordable apartments for people earning no more than\n30%, 40% and 50% of AMI or apartments for the formerly homeless; (2) the\nMixed-Middle Income program (\"M2\"), which provides apartments for people\nat a mix of income, 11.-K ludin Iffrme people, moderate-income people\nearning 80-100% of AMI and middle-income people' earning '1 6116 (A, of\nAMI; (3) the \"Mix and Match\" program, which provides apartments for\nlow-income and moderate- or middle-income people in the same project;\n(4) the \"50/30/20\" program, which provides apartments for low- and\nmoderate-income -people in projects where half the project is rented at\nthe market rate; (5) ,the Mitchell-Lama Reinvestment Program which\nanchors the affordability of the remaining Mitchell-Lama apartments; and\n(6) the Neighborhood Pillars program. In addition, HDC has launched a\nseries of other initiatives that use the corporation's bonding authori-\nty, including programs using taxable bonds and recycled tax-exempt\nbonds.\n\nThis legislation will enable HDC to continue to run programs that devel-\nop and maintain affordable housing in New York City and plan for future\nfinancings.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
